# XGSPON Feature Development (Oreo)

When given a **feature description**, follow this workflow.

---

## 1. Task breakdown

| Phase | Deliverable |
|-------|-------------|
| **Framework design** | Affected repos, YANG/proto changes, API boundaries, call flow, DbgCli plan |
| **Coding** | Files/functions, `Oreo/` branch, DbgCli commands if needed |
| **Review** | Self-review; CR; commit message format |
| **Compile & verify** | `bitbake <recipe> -c compile -f`; OLT/DbgCli verify when applicable |

---

## 2. Framework design (brief)

Document scope, data model (`xpon-yang` / `xpon-protos`), layer ownership, build order (`xpon-yang` → `xpon-protos` → `xpon-libs` → `netconf-polt` → `xpon-apps`), and planned DbgCli commands for new runtime state.

---

## 3. Git branch policy (mandatory)

- **Forbidden**: develop or `git push` on **`hank-dev`**.
- **Required**: branch name starts with **`Oreo/`** (e.g. `Oreo/XGS-9899-feature`).
- Check `git branch --show-current` in **each** affected repo under `workspace/sources/`.

---

## 4. Read-only repos (mandatory — no edits)

| Directory | Use | Forbidden |
|-----------|-----|-----------|
| `sw-bcm686olt` | Learn BAL/host_driver API | Edit / commit / push |
| `libyang` | Learn YANG parser | Edit / commit / push |
| `sysrepo` | Learn datastore behavior | Edit / commit / push |

Implement in editable repos: `netconf-polt/balapi`, `netconf-polt`, `xpon-yang`.

---

## 5. Code style & DbgCli

**Style:** read surrounding code before edit; minimal diff.

**DbgCli (DBC)** — add when feature has inspectable runtime state:

| Component | File |
|-----------|------|
| NETCONF/bbf-xpon | `netconf-polt/.../bbf-debug.c` |
| protocol-handler | `xpon-apps/protocol-handler/common/src/ph_dbg.c` |

Handler: validate `argc`/`argv`; empty input → print Usage, return `RET_OK` (**no SIGSEGV**). Register in `gCmsDbgCliCommands[]` + update help text. API: `xpon-libs/debug/include/dbg.h`.

---

## 6. Review checklist

Logic / memory safety / YANG alignment → code style → DbgCli → CR text → commit message → cross-repo consistency. Do not commit unless user asks.

**netconf-polt concurrency:** identify execution plane (config change / oper-get pull / oper-push / indication / metrics). Operational `sr_set_item*` / `sr_apply_changes` must run **outside** all L1–L7 locks (OPR-1). See `netconf-polt-mutex-rule.mdc` (auto-applied on `netconf-polt/**`).

---

## 7. Minimal diff for review (mandatory on refactors)

Goal: reviewer sees **only the intentional logic change** (lock, data structure, API), not a reformatting avalanche.

### 7.1 Preserve control-flow shape

- Keep existing patterns: `do { ... } while (0)`, early `break`/`return`, brace placement.
- Do **not** flatten `do-while` into straight-line `if/return` unless the logic truly changes.

### 7.2 Do not re-indent unchanged bodies

When wrapping code (new lock, outer `for`, guard macro):

- **Only add/remove/replace the wrapper lines** (lock, loop header, list index).
- Leave the **inner block at its original column** — even if outer braces look misaligned.
- Indentation at the wrapper may violate local style; **review-friendly diff beats brace aesthetics**.
- Prefer **`XPON_VANI_RUNTIME_GUARD(pon_ni)`** over explicit `bcmos_mutex_lock`/`unlock` pairs for short critical sections — avoids extra ±2 lines and keeps intent clear. Minimal-diff targets **body re-indent**, not avoiding the guard macro.

Example — per-PON lock on an existing `STAILQ_FOREACH`:

```c
    unsigned int local_skip_count = metrics_cache_bal_onu_poll_skip;
    int _pon;
    for (_pon = 0; _pon < XPON_VANI_NUM_PON_BUCKETS && n < snap_cap; _pon++)
    {
        XPON_VANI_CONFIG_GUARD(_pon);
        STAILQ_FOREACH_SAFE(hdr, &v_ani_list[_pon], next, hdr_tmp)
    {   /* ← opening brace stays at original column; body lines unchanged */
        va = (xpon_v_ani *)hdr;
        ...
    }
    }
```

Diff should show ~5 changed lines; the foreach **body must not appear** in the diff.

### 7.3 Prefer inline wrapper over helper extraction

- Do **not** move an unchanged loop into a new static helper just to shrink the parent function — that hides the real change across two hunks.
- Extract a helper only when it **reduces duplication** or **shortens a critical section** (e.g. snapshot-then-unlock before slow I/O), not for cosmetic diff layout.

### 7.4 Verify before hand-off

```bash
git diff <base> -- <file>   # body of refactored loop should be absent from diff
```

If many unchanged lines show as modified, fix indentation/wrapping before requesting review.

